Scope / public contract
- Ten tools: message_send, message_list, message_receipts, message_ack; ch_ls, ch_subscribe, ch_unsubscribe, ch_link, ch_pause, ch_grant.
- Worker catalogue has12 tools, Oracle15 (five existing reads plus the allowed additions). ch_link/pause/grant are Oracle-only in discovery AND dispatch. seat_mute/unmute, guarded input, spawn and task/artifact tools remain unavailable.
- Actor remains capability/live-generation/registry/epoch derived. Caller role, sender, operator, generation and swarm substitutions are refused. Every W2.4 request uses operator=false.
- Existing W2.4 semantics are preserved: default lists are caller inbox/outbox even for Oracle; explicit Oracle channel reads are own-swarm scoped. Sender and own-swarm Oracle have existing full receipt visibility; ordinary recipients see their own receipt rows. Ack is the admitted recipient/agent's explicit acknowledgement. It does not create an unread cursor or prove native model consumption.
- A mutation takes a model-visible op_id argument. It binds existing Call.op_id, is stripped before typed dispatch, is compatible with old _meta, and rejects argument/_meta mismatch. Message ID is that operation ID.
- Intent and audit commit BEFORE dispatch; the SQLite transaction is released before W2.4 writes. Outcome and audit then commit atomically. Completed retries return the retained result; argument/authority conflicts refuse dispatch; an uncertain/crashed operation remains UNKNOWN and never redispatches automatically.
- W2.4 read permissions are checked before cached results are exposed. A current denial is audited/returned without disclosing or replacing an earlier authorized result.

